From d81c35927cdbc35ab1b2b71a40e96fa554c49b70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:24:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/20] dos: Count client CREATE cells over the circuit
 creation rate

The network team asks whether a circuit flood comes from many addresses
or from a few. DoSCircuitCreation answers only for addresses it marks,
and it marks an address only once its bucket is empty and it holds at
least DoSCircuitCreationMinConnections connections, so addresses that
each keep one connection are never counted, however fast they create
circuits.

Count, as tor_relay_dos_total{type="circuit_over_rate"}, every CREATE
cell from a client channel that finds its address's circuit bucket
empty, whether or not the address is marked. Count also, as
tor_relay_dos_total{type="circuit_untracked"}, the CREATE cells from
client addresses that have no geoip entry, which DoSCircuitCreation
neither limits nor counts: tor drops an address's entry a day or two
after its last new connection, even while older connections from it stay
open, and on a bridge, a client that connects over a pluggable transport
has an entry only under the transport's name, which DoSCircuitCreation
doesn't look up.

In T seconds, an address can send at most DoSCircuitCreationBurst +
T * DoSCircuitCreationRate CREATE cells before its bucket is empty, so
over T seconds, (client CREATE cells - circuit_over_rate -
circuit_untracked) / (T * DoSCircuitCreationRate +
DoSCircuitCreationBurst) is a lower bound on how many tracked client
addresses sent CREATE cells. Both count only while
DoSCircuitCreationEnabled is set, as the bucket does.

Part of #41339.
